    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0085" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00/706</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Chief of the Division
                        of European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-03-18">March 18, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Soviet Chargé d’Affaires<anchor n="22" xml:id="d69-f1"/> in the course of a
                    conversation said that bad as the situation in Europe was, he felt there were
                    some bright spots. The first was the statement of Mr. Welles,<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d69-f2"/> and the second, the changed attitude of Mr.
                        Chamberlain.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d69-f3"/> None the less, he did not see
                    any possibility of ultimate pacification until certain Western Powers gave up
                    their wish thinking that Hitler’s idea was ultimately to move against Soviet
                    Russia. Such was not the case.</p>
                <p>The Chargé felt that the next move of Germany would be in the direction of
                    Rumania, with a view to making certain of basic raw supplies. Thereupon he
                    believed that Germany would turn west, chiefly against France, realizing that
                    never again would she have as good an opportunity to strike as at present. He
                    attached considerable importance to the fact that France was now effectively
                    surrounded by hostile countries on three sides and that there could be no
                    repetition of 1870 or 1914, when the Government chose to move to Bordeaux.</p>
                <p>He repeated Litvinov’s<anchor n="25" xml:id="d69-f4"/> dictum that peace was
                    indivisible, and said that ever since the fall of Barcelona<anchor n="26"
                        xml:id="d69-f5"/> his Government had been anticipating a heavy
                    German-Italian drive.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Pierrepont Moffat</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d69-f1" n="22" xml:id="d69-f1-content">
                <p>Constantine A. Oumansky.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d69-f2" n="23" xml:id="d69-f2-content">
                <p>See statement issued to the press by the Acting Secretary of State, March 17,
                    1939, <ref target="#pg49">p. 49</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d69-f3" n="24" xml:id="d69-f3-content">
                <p>British Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d69-f4" n="25" xml:id="d69-f4-content">
                <p>Maxim Litvinov, Soviet Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d69-f5" n="26" xml:id="d69-f5-content">
                <p>For information concerning collapse of Spanish Loyalist resistance in Catalan
                    area, see telegram No. 1206, February 5, 1939, 8 p.m., from the Counselor of
                    Embassy in Spain, printed in <ref target="frus1939v02">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>
                    </ref>, section entitled “The Spanish Civil War: I. International Political
                    Aspects.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
